.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_stream_create (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, uint32_t format, uint32_t channels, uint32_t rate, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_stream)"

.PP
Opens a stream the host writes samples into\&. What internet radio, a speech synthesiser and anything generated all want\&. The samples are converted and resampled to whatever the device asked for, so a host writes what it has rather than what the hardware wants\&.
.PP
Nothing is heard until schultz_audio_stream_play\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIformat\fP SCHULTZ_AUDIO_S16 or SCHULTZ_AUDIO_F32\&. 
.br
\fIchannels\fP One for mono, two for stereo\&. More is allowed\&. 
.br
\fIrate\fP Samples per second, such as 44100 or 48000\&. 
.br
\fIout_stream\fP Receives the stream\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE when no device would open\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_stream_write (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP stream, const void * bytes, uint64_t length)"

.PP
Hands the stream more samples\&. Copied, so the memory is the host's again the moment this returns\&. There is no limit but memory: a host that writes faster than the device plays is what schultz_audio_stream_queued is for\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIstream\fP A stream from schultz_audio_stream_create\&. 
.br
\fIbytes\fP The samples\&. Must not be NULL when length is not zero\&. 
.br
\fIlength\fP How many bytes, not how many samples\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint64_t schultz_audio_stream_queued (const \fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP stream)"

.PP
Returns how many bytes are waiting to be played\&. Counted as they were written, in the format the stream was created with, because nothing is converted until the device asks for it\&. So a stream written at 22050 and played at 48000 reports what was written, and dividing by the rate, the channel count and the sample size gives how many seconds of sound are still to come\&. That is what makes this usable as a clock as well as for deciding when to write more\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. NULL yields zero\&. 
.br
\fIstream\fP A stream from schultz_audio_stream_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Bytes not yet played\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_stream_play (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP stream)"

.PP
Starts or resumes a stream\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIstream\fP A stream from schultz_audio_stream_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_stream_pause (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP stream)"

.PP
Stops a stream where it is, keeping what is queued\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIstream\fP A stream from schultz_audio_stream_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_stream_is_playing (const \fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP stream)"

.PP
Reports whether a stream is running\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. NULL yields zero\&. 
.br
\fIstream\fP A stream from schultz_audio_stream_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Nonzero when it is playing\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_stream_clear (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP stream)"

.PP
Throws away everything queued but not yet played\&. What a station change wants: the seconds already buffered belong to the thing that is no longer playing\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIstream\fP A stream from schultz_audio_stream_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_stream_set_volume (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP stream, float volume)"

.PP
Sets one stream's own volume, from zero to one\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIstream\fP A stream from schultz_audio_stream_create\&. 
.br
\fIvolume\fP Zero for silence, one for as written\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "float schultz_audio_stream_volume (const \fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP stream)"

.PP
Returns one stream's own volume\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. NULL yields zero\&. 
.br
\fIstream\fP A stream from schultz_audio_stream_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The volume last set, or one\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_stream_destroy (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP stream)"

.PP
Closes a stream and drops whatever it still held\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIstream\fP A stream from schultz_audio_stream_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_encoder_create (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, uint32_t channels, uint32_t bitrate, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_encoder)"

.PP
Opens an encoder: samples in, Opus packets out\&. The sound half of recording rather than playing\&. Nothing here sends anything anywhere: the packets come back to the caller, which decides what they are for\&. A transport is a different problem\&.
.PP
Opus, because it is the audio codec that can be shipped without a patent licence and the best of them at the bitrates a call uses\&. It is already vendored for playback, so encoding adds nothing to the binary but the encoder itself\&.
.PP
\fBForty eight thousand samples a second\&.\fP That is what Opus works in and what everything else in this toolkit hands about, so there is nothing to resample and no rate to choose\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIchannels\fP One for mono, two for stereo\&. 
.br
\fIbitrate\fP The target, in bits a second\&. Six thousand to half a million; a voice call is usually twenty four thousand\&. 
.br
\fIout_encoder\fP Receives the encoder\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE when the encoder will not start\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_encoder_write (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP encoder, const float * samples, uint64_t frames)"

.PP
Hands the encoder more samples\&. Float samples, interleaved when there is more than one channel, at forty eight thousand a second\&. Copied, so the memory is the caller's again when this returns\&.
.PP
Opus codes in fixed lengths of time, so samples are held here until there are enough for a packet\&. Twenty milliseconds is the length used, which is 960 sample frames: writing fewer produces nothing yet, and writing more produces several packets\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIencoder\fP An encoder from schultz_audio_encoder_create\&. 
.br
\fIsamples\fP The samples\&. Must not be NULL when frames is not zero\&. 
.br
\fIframes\fP How many sample frames, not how many samples: a stereo pair is one frame\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_encoder_read_packet (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP encoder, const void ** out_bytes, uint64_t * out_length, uint64_t * out_when_ns)"

.PP
Takes the next packet the encoder has ready\&. Call it until it answers SCHULTZ_ERR_EXHAUSTED, which means there are not yet enough samples for another\&.
.PP
The bytes belong to the encoder and stay valid until the next call to this or to schultz_audio_encoder_write\&. A caller that keeps one copies it\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIencoder\fP An encoder from schultz_audio_encoder_create\&. 
.br
\fIout_bytes\fP Receives the packet\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_length\fP Receives how many bytes\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_when_ns\fP Receives how far into the sound the packet starts, in nanoseconds from the first sample written\&. May be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE or SCHULTZ_ERR_EXHAUSTED when there is nothing ready\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_encoder_set_bitrate (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP encoder, uint32_t bitrate)"

.PP
Changes the target bitrate, from the next packet onward\&. The same handle congestion control needs on the video side, for the same reason\&. Sound is the half worth protecting when the network gets bad: a call whose picture stutters is annoying, and one whose sound breaks up is over\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIencoder\fP An encoder from schultz_audio_encoder_create\&. 
.br
\fIbitrate\fP The new target, in bits a second\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_encoder_destroy (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP encoder)"

.PP
Closes an encoder and releases what it held\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIencoder\fP An encoder from schultz_audio_encoder_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_packet_decoder_create (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, uint32_t channels, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_decoder)"

.PP
Opens a packet decoder: Opus packets in, samples out\&. The other end of schultz_audio_encoder_create, and what a receiver at the far end of a transport needs\&. Packets arrive, samples come back, and the caller plays them or does whatever else it likes with them\&.
.PP
\fBNot the same thing as schultz_audio_decoder_create\fP, which is a player for a whole encoded file -- an MP3, an Ogg, a FLAC -- arriving in pieces, and which plays it itself\&. This takes bare Opus packets in the order they were produced, gives back the samples, and plays nothing\&.
.PP
Forty eight thousand samples a second, like the encoder, because that is what Opus works in\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIchannels\fP One for mono, two for stereo\&. Must match what the far end encoded\&. 
.br
\fIout_decoder\fP Receives the decoder\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_packet_decoder_write (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP decoder, const void * bytes, uint64_t length)"

.PP
Hands the decoder one packet\&. Packets must arrive in the order they were produced\&. What comes out is taken with schultz_audio_packet_decoder_read\&.
.PP
A packet that is lost is not the end: pass NULL with a length of zero and Opus works out something plausible to cover the gap, which is what keeps a call going through a bad moment rather than clicking\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIdecoder\fP A decoder from schultz_audio_packet_decoder_create\&. 
.br
\fIbytes\fP The packet, or NULL for one that was lost\&. 
.br
\fIlength\fP How many bytes, or zero for one that was lost\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNREADABLE when the packet could not be decoded\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_packet_decoder_read (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP decoder, const float ** out_samples, uint64_t * out_frames)"

.PP
Takes the samples from the last packet written\&. Float samples, interleaved when there is more than one channel, at forty eight thousand a second -- the same shape schultz_audio_stream_write takes, so a decoded packet can go straight out to the speakers\&.
.PP
The samples belong to the decoder and stay valid until the next call to schultz_audio_packet_decoder_write\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
