.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_decoder_create (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_decoder)"

.PP
Opens a decoder the host pushes an encoded file into, a piece at a time\&. What internet radio wants\&. A stream takes samples and this takes the file itself -- MP3, Ogg Vorbis, FLAC, WAV -- in whatever chunks it arrives in, and works them out as it plays\&.
.PP
The rule is the same as everywhere else here: the host writes, and nothing calls the host back\&. The decoder reads from what has been written, on its own thread, and an empty buffer means 'not yet' rather than 'the end', so a station that is slow for a moment does not stop\&.
.PP
\fBWrite some bytes before playing\&.\fP The decoder reads the start of the file to see what kind it is, so schultz_audio_decoder_play on an empty decoder has nothing to decide from\&. A few kilobytes is plenty\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_decoder\fP Receives the decoder\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE when no device would open\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_audio_decoder_write (\fBschultz_audio\fP * audio, \fBschultz_handle\fP decoder, const void * bytes, uint64_t length)"

.PP
Hands the decoder more of the file\&. Copied, so the memory is the host's again when this returns\&. What has been read is dropped as it goes, so a station may play for hours without the memory growing\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIaudio\fP The system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIdecoder\fP A decoder from schultz_audio_decoder_create\&. 
.br
\fIbytes\fP The next piece of the file\&. Must not be NULL when length is not zero\&. 
.br
\fIlength\fP How many bytes\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

